findings.body:
In `Action.fail()`, the PR changed the first emit (`prefect.automation.action.triggered`) to use `self._resulting_related_resources` and moved `related_resources` onto `action.failed`. In the base commit it was the other way around: `action.triggered` used `related_resources` (which carries the `triggering-event` link) and `action.failed` used `_resulting_related_resources`.

After this change, the failure path's `action.triggered` drops the `triggering-event` link that worked before. It also never gets the new `automation-triggered-event` link, even though `test_failure_events_include_automation_triggered_event_link` asserts that it does. The docs state that action events link back to their source events, so the failure path breaks that promise.

::diff{file="src/prefect/server/events/actions.py" lines="197-213"}

::suggestion{file="src/prefect/server/events/actions.py" lines="202-202"}
                    related=related_resources,

`primary_state_change_event_id` prefers the Triggered event over Resolved. `act()` then passes that single ID to every source action, including `actions_on_resolve`. When a firing carries both states, `actions_on_resolve` actions get `automation-triggered-event` pointing at the `automation.triggered` event, even though `triggering_event` for those actions is the `automation.resolved` event.

This contradicts the field description in `schemas/automations.py` ("the automation.triggered or automation.resolved event that prompted this action") and the docs table. Resolve actions should link to the resolved event. The PR's tests cover only the Triggered path, so this is not caught.

::code{file="src/prefect/server/events/triggers.py" lines="466-470"}

::callout{type="info"}
The fix is to pass each group's own state-change event ID rather than one shared primary ID.

::

Both trigger service tests add `automation_triggered_event_id=parsed.automation_triggered_event_id` to the expected `TriggeredAction`, where `parsed` is the model parsed from the published message. The equality check therefore never validates the new field's value, and the proactive case can't reveal a wrong ID. The test would still pass if `act()` set the wrong event ID or none at all.

Assert the expected value explicitly. Either check that it equals the ID of the Triggered state-change event the test expects, or check it is not None for the reactive case. Otherwise these tests give false confidence about the new linkage. The proactive test should also check the expected `None` or non-None value directly after the fix for the validator.

::diff{file="tests/events/server/triggers/test_service.py" lines="57-61"}
